//! lotus/decimate.rye -- ALES83: the sample-rate decimator -- hold each sample across a run, crushing TIME rather than AMPLITUDE. The bit-crusher
//! (ALES81) drops the low bits, lowering a sample's RESOLUTION while every sample still keeps its own moment in time; the decimator shapes the OTHER
//! axis -- it holds one sample across a whole run, so a stream that entered at the full rate leaves at half, or a quarter, or a sixteenth: the aliased,
//! stepped, sample-and-hold voice of an early sampler read too slow, a lo-fi converter, a digital signal starved of TIME instead of resolution. The
//! bit-crush is the decimator's twin -- one crushes the value axis, the other the time axis, and together they name the two ways a converter goes coarse.
//!
//! The crux is that a DECIMATION BY `hold` IS A ZERO-ORDER HOLD: partition the span into runs of `hold` samples anchored at `start`, and pin every
//! sample in a run to the run's FIRST sample -- its anchor. hold = 1 is the IDENTITY (each run is one sample, held to itself). hold = 2 keeps every
//! other sample and repeats it once; hold = 4 keeps one in four. This is the family's FIRST map that is not memoryless PER SAMPLE -- an output equals
//! its run's anchor, a NEIGHBOR, not only its own input -- yet it is memoryless PER RUN: each run depends only on its own anchor, so decimating a span
//! whose run boundaries align equals decimating it whole. And it is IDEMPOTENT -- a run already held is held to the same anchor, so decimate(decimate(x))
//! = decimate(x).
//!
//! The decimator writes only sample values that ALREADY LIVE in the clip -- it copies an anchor across its run and computes NO new number -- so every
//! written value is a legal i16 by construction and there is no saturate to do: the map narrows time, never magnitude. In place it is safe left to
//! right -- a run's anchor sits at or before every index it fills (anchor ≤ i), and the anchor is written to ITSELF (a no-op) before any later index in
//! the run reads it, so buf[anchor] always holds the original anchor value while the run is filled. The last run may be PARTIAL (when the span is not a
//! whole multiple of `hold`); a partial run holds its anchor across however many samples remain, exactly as a full run would.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES83.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-213107_fill-ales83-lotus-sample-rate-decimator.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_min/max, max_clip. It carries NO pre-gain and NO grid -- like the bit-crush it is a pure
//! resolution map, here on the TIME axis; a keeper who wants a boosted or clipped decimation runs lotus/drive.rye (ALES78) or lotus/crush.rye (ALES81)
//! then decimate over the same span, all in-place span maps that compose.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate -- the `hold` is a sample count (unity = 1), the shape a zero-order hold, instantaneous per run (no
//! interpolation, no anti-aliasing filter -- a raw decimation aliases in the i16 domain exactly as any hardware sample-and-hold does). One divide, one
//! copy per sample; the decimator only repeats existing samples, so the output fits the i16 rail by construction.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/decimate.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");

pub const decimate_version = "20260814.213107";

/// The widest hold the i16 timeline holds. A decimation names a run of between one sample (the identity) and the whole timeline's worth. Past
/// max_clip there is no span that long to hold across; below one there is no run -- both refuse BadHold.
pub const max_hold: u32 = timeline.max_clip;

/// The sample-rate decimator's faults, one consistent name each. BadRange coincides by name with the sibling drives and ALES2's timeline (Zig merges
/// error sets by name), so a composer sees one consistent span fault. BadHold is the decimator's own -- the time axis no other DRIVE member names.
pub const DecimateError = error{
    /// A hold of zero (names no run) or past the timeline's width (no span that long to hold across) -- refused, no write.
    BadHold,
    /// A span outside the current samples -- refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Decimate `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip` by `hold`: partition the span into runs of `hold` samples anchored at `start`, and
/// pin every sample in a run to the run's first sample (its anchor) -- a zero-order hold. After the call every sample equals its run's anchor, its
/// value is one that already lived in the clip (a copy, never a new number), and each run depends only on its own anchor (the decimator is memoryless
/// per run). `hold = 1` is the identity (each run is one sample). Refuses BadHold on a hold outside [1, max_clip] and BadRange on a span outside the
/// samples -- each BEFORE any write, so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only -- never the length.
pub fn decimate(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, hold: u32) DecimateError!void {
    // invariant: the hold is legal -- at least one sample (a run of one is the identity) and no more than the timeline's width. Checked BEFORE any write.
    if (hold < 1) return error.BadHold;
    if (hold > max_hold) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples -- checked without underflow (count vs the remainder), exactly as the sibling drives.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: with hold ≥ 1 the run index below never divides by zero, and every anchor sits at or before its own index, so the in-place hold
    // reads an original value.
    assert(hold >= 1 and start + count <= clip.len);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        // The run's anchor: floor k to a whole multiple of `hold` (k and hold both non-negative, so divTrunc is the floor) and step forward from
        // `start`. anchor ≤ i always, and anchor == i exactly at each run's first sample -- the sample the run holds.
        const anchor: u32 = start + @divTrunc(k, hold) * hold;
        // invariant: the anchor lies within the span and at or before this index -- so buf[anchor] is a legal, already-original sample (it was written
        // to itself before any later index in the run reads it).
        assert(anchor >= start and anchor <= i and anchor < start + count);
        const held: i16 = clip.buf[anchor];
        // invariant: the held value is a legal i16 -- it is an existing audio sample copied across the run, never a wide intermediate, so the write
        // fits the rail BY CONSTRUCTION and no saturate is owed (the decimator narrows TIME, never magnitude).
        assert(held >= timeline.sample_min and held <= timeline.sample_max);
        clip.buf[i] = held;
    }
    // invariant: a decimation changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: hold = 1 is the IDENTITY (each run is one sample); ZERO-ORDER HOLD by hand (hold 3 over a ramp, the last run PARTIAL); EVERY sample
//     equals its RUN ANCHOR and the map is IDEMPOTENT (a held run holds to the same anchor); hold ≥ count holds the WHOLE span at the first sample;
//     the RAIL is preserved (a decimation only copies existing samples, so every output is a legal i16); the SPAN discipline holds; and each fault --
//     BadHold, BadRange -- refuses by name, the clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. hold = 1 IS THE IDENTITY -- each run is one sample held to itself, so the decimation returns the clip byte-for-byte. The map generalizes the
    //    untouched clip, the way ALES81's bits = 16 reproduces the whole-resolution signal.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1, -1, 12345, -12345, 32767, timeline.sample_min, 0, 255 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.OneNotIdentity;
    }

    // 2. THE ZERO-ORDER HOLD BY HAND -- hold = 3 over a seven-sample ramp, the runs read directly. Runs of three anchored at 0: [10,20,30]->10,
    //    [40,50,60]->40, and the last run [70] is PARTIAL (one sample), held to its own anchor 70. A hold of three keeps one sample in three and
    //    repeats it across its run.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 3);
        const want = [_]i16{ 10, 10, 10, 40, 40, 40, 70 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.HoldWrong;
    }

    // 3. EVERY SAMPLE EQUALS ITS RUN ANCHOR, AND THE MAP IS IDEMPOTENT -- at hold = 4 each sample equals the first of its run of four, and decimating
    //    the already-decimated clip a second time changes nothing (a held run holds to the same anchor).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 4);
        const once = clip.samples();
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < once.len) : (k += 1) {
            const anchor: u32 = @divTrunc(k, 4) * 4;
            if (once[k] != once[anchor]) return error.NotHeldToAnchor;
        }
        var again = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&again, once);
        try decimate(&again, 0, again.len, 4);
        if (!eq(again.samples(), once)) return error.NotIdempotent;
    }

    // 4. A HOLD WIDER THAN THE SPAN holds the WHOLE span at the first sample -- one run covers everything, so every sample becomes the anchor. A hold
    //    equal to or past the count is the coarsest decimation the span holds: a single held value.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 7, 11, 13, 17, 19 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 100); // 100 ≥ 5: one run over the whole span
        const want = [_]i16{ 7, 7, 7, 7, 7 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.WholeSpanWrong;
    }

    // 5. THE RAIL IS PRESERVED -- a decimation only COPIES existing samples, so every output is a legal i16 whatever the rail. Decimate a signal that
    //    rides both rails; the extremes survive intact wherever a run anchors on them.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, 32767, timeline.sample_min, 32767, timeline.sample_min, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2); // runs of two anchored at 0: each pair held to its first
        const want = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.RailNotHeld;
        const out = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) if (out[i] < timeline.sample_min or out[i] > timeline.sample_max) return error.RailCrossed;
    }

    // 6. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE -- decimating a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the whole
    //    suite keeps. Hold the middle three of seven at hold = 3 (one run); the first and last two are untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try decimate(&clip, 2, 3, 3); // indices 2,3,4 held to 300 — one run anchored at 2
        const want = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 300, 300, 600, 700 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 7. A ZERO / OVER-WIDE HOLD refuses BadHold; a SPAN past the end refuses BadRange -- each before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, 10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongZeroHold;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroHoldTouched;
        if (decimate(&clip, 0, clip.len, max_hold + 1)) |_| return error.WideHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongWideHold;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedWideHoldTouched;
        if (decimate(&clip, 1, 3, 2)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-decimate: the SAMPLE-RATE DECIMATOR — hold each sample across a run, crushing TIME rather than AMPLITUDE. The bit-crusher " ++
        "(ALES81) drops the low bits, lowering RESOLUTION while every sample keeps its own moment; the decimator shapes the OTHER axis — it holds one " ++
        "sample across a whole run, so a stream entered at the full rate leaves at half, a quarter, a sixteenth: the aliased sample-and-hold voice of " ++
        "an early converter read too slow. A decimation by `hold` is a ZERO-ORDER HOLD — partition the span into runs of `hold` anchored at start and " ++
        "pin every sample in a run to its FIRST (the anchor). hold = 1 is the identity (each run is one sample); the zero-order hold by hand at hold " ++
        "3 over a ramp [10,20,30,40,50,60,70] gives [10,10,10,40,40,40,70], the last run PARTIAL; every sample equals its run anchor and the map is " ++
        "IDEMPOTENT (a held run holds to the same anchor); a hold wider than the span holds the whole span at the first sample; the rail is preserved " ++
        "(a decimation only COPIES existing samples, so every output is a legal i16); the span discipline holds; a zero or over-wide hold refuses " ++
        "BadHold, a span past the end BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched. The decimator is the family's FIRST map that is not memoryless per " ++
        "SAMPLE (an output equals its run's anchor, a neighbor) yet memoryless per RUN (each run depends only on its own anchor, so a run-aligned " ++
        "block decimation equals the whole); it writes only values that already live in the clip, so no saturate is owed; one divide, one copy per " ++
        "sample; no pre-gain, no grid, no interpolation, no anti-aliasing, no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample " ++
        "rate. The DRIVE family now holds six shapes — the wall, the shoulder, the mirror, the coarse grid, the uneven rail, and the stepped time; " ++
        "the bit-crush's twin on the time axis is met, and the waveshaper family is whole\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
